# Worktree-per-Agent Convention

This repo is set up for **multiple Cursor Cloud Agents running in parallel**, each in its own git worktree. Treat your worktree as an isolated sandbox.

## Branch naming
- `agent/<short-task-slug>` for autonomous agent work (e.g. `agent/twitter-rate-limit`, `agent/notion-schema-v2`).
- One branch = one PR = one logical change. Don't pile unrelated edits on top.

## Boundaries
- Never `git push --force` to `main` or to another agent's branch.
- Never delete branches you didn't create.
- If you need to read another agent's in-progress work, fetch and read — don't merge into your branch without being asked.

## Conflict policy
- If your branch falls behind `main`, prefer `git rebase origin/main` over a merge commit.
- If a rebase has non-trivial conflicts in files you didn't author, stop and ask the user instead of guessing the intent.

## PR hygiene
- Title: imperative, ≤ 70 chars, no scope prefix unless the repo already uses one.
- Body: what changed, why, and any follow-ups left for another agent.
- If your change touches the public CLI (`[project.scripts]`) or `.env.example`, call it out explicitly in the PR body.

## Coordination
- Long-running tasks (multi-hour autonomous runs) should leave a status note in the PR description so other agents/humans can see what's in flight.
- If you discover work that should be handed to a different agent, open a separate issue rather than expanding your own scope.
